# Change Audit Log Rule

## When to log

After completing any task that touches the repo, append an entry to `CHANGE_LOG.md` (repo root).

**Always log:**
- Multi-file edits
- Behavior-changing logic (calculations, auth, routing, email, API shape)
- Any file delete, rename, or move
- Security / auth / env var changes
- New features or endpoints

**May skip:** trivial one-line typo/copy fixes that have zero behavior impact.

## Where

Append to **`CHANGE_LOG.md`** at the **repo root** (not inside `backend/` or `frontend/`).
New entries go at the **bottom** (append-only; newest = last).

## Entry template

Separate each entry with `---`. Fill every field — no skipping.

```
---
**Timestamp:** 2026-04-10T14:30:00Z
**Summary:** One short line of intent.
**Files:**
- path/relative/to/repo/root.ext
- another/file.ext
**Details:**
What changed — API shape, env vars added/removed, logic that changed, why.
No large code blobs; reference the file + line number instead.
**Undo / Rollback:**
- Uncommitted: `git restore <file>`
- Committed: `git revert <sha>` or manually revert <field> in <file> back to <prior value>.
```

## Git reminder

Git is the binary-exact source of truth. `CHANGE_LOG.md` is the human-readable narrative and pointer layer — both must stay in sync.
Commit `CHANGE_LOG.md` together with the code changes it describes.
